    "description": "Break the timeline into stages when a partner adds to or withdraws part of the investment.",
    "teachingApproach": "Treat every capital change as a new chapter in the same partner's timeline. Build a stage table mentally: old capital × old months, then new capital × new months, and add those products. Start with one change, then both partners changing, percentage changes and multi-year versions. The learner repeatedly sees why using the final capital for the whole year is the classic error.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 14, Partnership. Approved scope: Teach piecewise capital-time accounting for partners whose capital changes while the business continues. Cover a withdrawal after some months, an additional investment, several partners changing at the same milestone, percentage increases such as a 50% increase in capital, two-stage and three-stage timelines, and multi-year versions. Emphasize that each stage contributes capital × months and the partner's total earning weight is the sum of those stage products. Prepare learners for objective patterns with additions, partial withdrawals and complete withdrawals."
